tzsp-monitor -- INSTALL (TL;DR)
================================
Router: 192.168.88.1 (MikroTik, RouterOS 7.15+)   Monitor host: 192.168.0.1

Shortcut: steps 1-3 (and the checks in 4) are what the setup assistant asks:

     git clone <repo> tzsp-monitor && cd tzsp-monitor && python3 install.py

It writes .env, suricata.properties and properties.yml, tests the router login
read-only and prints the RouterOS commands still missing. By hand instead:

1. Host (192.168.0.1): Docker + compose plugin, then

     git clone <repo> tzsp-monitor && cd tzsp-monitor
     cp suricata.properties.example suricata.properties   # BEFORE first `up`
     printf 'GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_USER=admin\nGF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme\n' > properties.yml

2. .env (git-ignored, next to docker-compose.yml): set your NIC

     echo SNIFF_INTERFACE=eth0 >> .env

3. suricata.properties -- the lines that matter:

     mikrotik.host        = 192.168.88.1
     mikrotik.user        = ids-responder
     mikrotik.password    = <secret>
     suricata.home_net    = [192.168.0.0/16]
     dns_filter.base_url  = http://192.168.0.1:8041

4. Router (192.168.88.1):

     # mirror traffic to the monitor as TZSP
     /tool/sniffer set filter-interface=ether1 filter-stream=yes \
         streaming-enabled=yes streaming-server=192.168.0.1:37008
     /tool/sniffer start
     # REST API + a user for the responder / dns-filter
     /ip/service enable www-ssl
     /user/group add name=ids policy=read,write,api,rest-api
     /user add name=ids-responder group=ids password=<secret>
     # the list is inert until a rule uses it (deliberately manual)
     /ip/firewall/raw add chain=prerouting src-address-list=suricata-blocked action=drop
     # DNS filtering: clients must use the router as resolver
     /ip/dns set allow-remote-requests=yes cache-size=65536

5. Start and check:

     docker compose up -d --build
     python3 tzsp-decap/selftest.py                   # IDS path end to end
     docker compose exec dns-filter python selftest.py
     docker compose logs --tail=3 tzsp-decap          # pps > 0 = mirror arrives

   Grafana: http://192.168.0.1:3000   (InfluxDB: :8086)

 / VXLAN-fed host instead of TZSP: skip the sniffer, point the vTAP at
UDP 4789 and run
     echo COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.vxlan.yml > .env
before step 5.

Open ports on the host: 37008/udp (mirror), 8041/tcp (router fetches lists),
3000/tcp (Grafana). Details and gotchas: CLAUDE.md.

Optional, full packet capture (pcap files of the mirror): off until switched on
in Grafana -> 3 · Security -> Packet Capture. Files land in ./pcap unless
     echo PCAP_DIR=/srv/pcap >> .env      # ideally a disk of its own
and the budget is pcap_dump.max_bytes in suricata.properties (default 10G).
